Inflation in Europe has slippery on food
Accelerating inflation in the euro area is mainly due to a rapid increase in food prices.
The price increase in November was higher than expected in the 13 countries of the eurozone, with an annual inflation rate of 3.1 per cent after 2.6 per cent in October, according to the EU’s Luxembourg Statistical Office.
An official forecast predicted only 3%, Bloomberg said.
The European Central Bank (ECB), unlike the U.S. central banks in the U.S. Central Bank (ECB), did not cut the base rate, saying that the reduction, together with rising raw material prices and benevising unemployment, would lead to the emergence of an inflationary spiral.
The ECB wants to keep the inflation rate below 2%.
According to an analyst, the ECB is trapped in the twenty-t-twenti-twenties: “inflation needs to be fooled” so that it doesn’t have to act later on this issue.
In Germany, an inflation rate of 3.3 per cent in November is a twelve-eight-eight-year record, rising from 3.6 in Spain.
Inflation in the euro area has risen by rising food and energy prices.
Future-selfish quotations have risen by 88 per cent, and soybean’s exchange rate has also reached its highest level since 1973.
Eat oil and dairy products also had to be paid significantly more in 2007.
The world market price of crude oil has risen by 52 percent this year, and last month a barrel of black gold cost almost 100 dollars.
According to the ECB, inflation rises from 2.1 to 2.5% this year in 2008, compared to 1.9 per cent in 2009.
According to analysts, the 12-month level of inflation will remain around 3 percent in the next 3-4 months, but the average annual rate will be 2.1 percent.
At the same time, it expects the ECB to lower interest rates twice in 2008.
Gallup sees a government-critical crisis
After a lengthy stagnation, Fidesz’s circle of support increased significantly by the beginning of the beginning of December, so it now has the largest camp since 2002, while Gallup has never previously measured such low socialist support of 13 per cent.
Due to the greater determination of the opposition electors, the difference would represent more than two-thirds (71 per cent) of MSZP’s (71 per cent) support compared to one-fifth (20 per cent).
According to MDF and SZD (SZDSZ), according to the survey, they are below the parliamentary threshold: their support within the entire population is two percent.
There are one percent more supporters of both camps.
The economic public mood has deteriorated even further to the previous low – it has never been so high, 41 per cent of those who judged the country’s economic situation to be particularly bad, while another 46 percent believe that the situation is rather bad.
Families now judge the change in their own financial situation after the hopeful prospects in the spring and the more hopeful in the spring.
For the next year, the majority of the population expects a further depreciation and only a one-tenth of an improvement.
In December, in all previous Gallup’s surveys, 71 per cent of the respondents believed that the prime minister would rather do his job poorly rather than the Prime Minister.
Ferenc Gyurcsány 13 per per cent of the respondents are confident or very confident, while Viktor Orban has 38 per cent of supporters.
Fifty-four per cent don’t trust the prime minister at all, with 27 per per cent of Fidesz’s president.
An indicator of the development of the political situation is how many parts of the society think that dissatisfaction with the prime minister can be dealt with without a change of government.
While 35 per per cent, after the riots in October last year, 35 per per cent of the government and the prime minister’s responsibility, at the moment this figure is only 20 per cent, while the relative majority (33 per cent) only sees the solution in new elections.
Interested in the referendum, 60 per cent of the respondents answering a question about their willingness to take a matter of the fact that they would surely go, and an additional 20 per cent would say that they would probably leave.
By way of comparison, only 55 per cent would take part in a “moociate election to be held on this Sunday” and an additional 15 per cent would consider their participation probable. The result of the referendum would not be questionable on the basis of current intentions.
Seventy-seven per cent would say no to tuition fees, 79 per cent for the visitation fees, and 84 per cent for the hospital’s daily allowance.
If we only look at the same proportions among those who promise to participate in the referendum, 81, 84 and 89 percent of the electorate would reject the introduction of tuition fees, exchange fees and hospital subsistence allowances.
Auchan is the most expensive, Tesco has risen by nearly one-fifth
The hypermarkets in the Budaörs trade trinits kept last year’s price increase of 16%, with the difference that VAT did not increase this year.
An article of the Daily Economy
On average this year, in the Budaörs trade triangle the usual Christmas basket of the Daily increased by an average of 16 percent, but in each hypermarket there was an increase of well over a thousand forints in value.
Since last December’s prices “survived” in grocery stores, there was no drastic increase compared to the previous high base.
However, it is obviously frightening for the consumers that what they were able to receive well below nine thousand forints last year does not even have access for ten thousand forints.
In the examined hypermarkets, the Daily basket exceeded the psychological limit of ten thousand forints by at least 91 forints.
The highest basket value was HUF 10,573.
(The basket cost 6,800 forints in 2003.)
The prices of Unicum and the live fish remained almost at last year’s level, but the turkey-breast, walnut and poppy was also very expensive.
The HCSO’s data was followed by the price of flour and bread in the stores, although we also found a kilos of bread in Auchan for 139 forints.
The competitor didn’t lower the price of the product below 200 forints.
It was also astonishing in this store that the price of the two-lit-litre-refundable Coca-Cola was below 200 forints, so they only asked for 257 forints for the product with a deposit fee.
At the same time, however, Auchan was the most expensive in the Daily’s basket this year, while Cora was the cheapest.
According to our calculations, compared to last year’s Christmas prices, Cora increased the prices in the Daily’s basket by 15 percent, while Tesco and Auchan increased by about 18-18 percent.
Comparing last year's predominant and this year’s cheapest basket value, the difference was 12%, while last year’s cheapest and this year’s most expensive was an increase of 21 percent.
On average there was an increase of 1,480.
“Last last year, the average price increase came at HUF 1,206, with an increase in VAT in the prices at last Christmas.
It was precisely this process that disrupted the commerce, apparently they couldn’t decide how much margin the stores would use for each product, because they couldn’t figure out the impact of the price change.
(If a product’s rotational speed is poorly designed by the trader, it can gain a significant profit at a margin adapted to traffic intensitation.)
Due to the VAT, last year’s increase in the price increase of the Daily’s basket was so extreme last year that the price difference was 1,7,020 forints more than in 2006, while the price difference was only HUF 844.
This extremism did not manifest itself this year, as the current most expensive was 1,630 forints more than last year, and the cheapest also increased by 1,284 forints.
Compared to the two Christmas seasons, there was a catastrophic price increase, in less than 20 months the daily consumer goods in hypermarkets increased by more than 30 percent.
By the way, this does not necessarily certify the data of the HCSO: the statistical office has an increase of more than 30 per cent over a period of 12 months for some basic foods.
For example, in addition to the flour for the egg.)
According to the shopping cart of Napi, this seems to be almost impossible for the latter, as the price level of the product, which was already quite expensive last year, produced a price increase of just over 10 per cent compared to the base in the worst-case scenario.
Flour deliverers seem to be in a stronger position than egg collectors, the drastic increase in price levels have also been scammed on multinationals.
In the commercial gold trinitism, the hyperes do not complain, they are constantly reporting a good year.
According to our experience, most of the buyers are no longer interested in prices in the three stores visited.
Quantities are purchased from everything, they carry durable foods in a flat-plate.
Serious queues were formed in front of the cash desks on Wednesday morning in all three department stores, because the number of visitors suddenly increased.
We know, however, that the turnover in the countryside is sparse, and several multinationals are complaining to the research companies that even their plans lag behind the revenue, not only in the case of food, but also in the case of durable consumer goods.
In many places they save on disinfectants
More than a thousand infections have been reported in hospitals this year, mainly due to the inappropriate use of antibiotics.
The hygienic conditions of the hospitals, the congestion of the beds and the congestion of the staff do not benefit from stopping the infections, according to the chief medical officer of the National Center of Epidemiology.
In many places they save on disinfectants as well.
MRSA has recently been diagnosed in the traumatology department of the János Hospital.
After a serious accident, the bacterium resistant to antibiotics was detected in a man who was infected with antibiotics.
The patient has been isolated.
A similar infection occurred earlier this year.
The injured person in a serious car accident was discharged from the hospital, but shortly thereafter he had to come back with an MRSA infection, and an important surgery has not been carried out ever since due to the bacterium in his/her body.
As of December 10 this year, 1,094/39 multi-resistant pathogen infections have been reported to the National Nosocomial Surveillance System, said Karolina Böröcz, chief medical officer of the National Epidemiological Center.
This means that there must have been so many infections caused by multi-resistant pathogens in hospitals, the rest is not known.
Approximately two-thirds (683 infections) were caused by MRSA (MRSA—ghetthicillin oxacillin-responsive Staphylococcus aureus.
Staphylococcus aureus (SA) bacterium is carried by one-on-a-half of humans without causing the disease.
Infections caused by SA (e.g. pharyngitis, tonsillitis, skin infections, wound infection) respond well to antibiotics and can be cured.
However, if antibiotics are used excessively or inappropriately, bacterium responding to anti-antibiotics develops a highly resilient bacterium to antibiotics, which spreads rapidly under improper hospital conditions.
Primarily when the immune system is temporary or persistently malfunction, it can cause serious hospital infections, such as wound infection or blood poisoning - sepsis.
According to the medical director of the János Hospital, there is nothing special about the MRSA infection, such infections belong to the daily practices of hospitals.
The infection should not confuse it with the pandemic, MRSA is only not there, said Tibor Nyulasi, where the presence of the pathogen is not investigated.
Contrastines for antibiotics
“Such patients are separated, the nursing staff treat the patient in rubber gloves and masks, and they have the right antibiotics for their treatment,” Tibor Nyulasi said.
Patients admitted to the hospital are already being tested to see MRSA present in their bodies, but the infection cannot always be prevented.
Sampling isn't fashionable
The vast majority of hospital infections caused by MRSA could be prevented, but the current domestic hospital conditions and conditions are not favorable in preventing the development and spread of multi-resistant pathogens, says Karolina Böröcz.
Since this infection typically occurs in hospitals and nursing homes, the hospitals should monitor the incoming patients, and patients carrying MRSA should be isolated immediately.
Microbiological Sampling is not fashionable in Hungarian hospitals.
This is also due to the fact that the National Health Insurance Treasury does not finance these investigations.
In this way, most of these cases will come to light if the patient has an undetectable MRSA-infect, the chief medical officer of the epidemiology center said.
The European Union has strict recommendations for the proper use of antibiotics in human medicine, and there are also domestic directives for the treatment of individual infections and pathologies.
It is necessary to ensure that anti-antibiotic therapy is targeted, i.e. microbiological studies should be carried out in order not to develop resistance to drugs, but hospitals do not always observe this.
Microbiological sampling is even rarer in outpatient and primary care, Karolina Böröcz said.
Sparing on disinfectants
Scientific evidence confirms that MRSA is most commonly distributed from one patient to another by mediating medical personnel.
That’s a very important role to be used for hand-washing, glove and mask, but there are also significant gaps in hospitals.
The MRSA spreads with droplet infection and contact, however, it is not always possible to isolate an infected patient under domestic hospital conditions.
Moreover, the wards are often crowded, the beds are often very close to each other, even though from an epidemiological point of view the patients should be at least 1.5 meters apart.
“Unfortunately, Karolina Böröcz also remarked that hospitals in an unfavourable financial situation save on disinfectants, there’s a shortage of nurses, there’s a lot of patients, so there’s an even better chance of developing infections.
According to Karolina Böröcz, where appropriate precautions are observed, there are demonstrably fewer such infections, or the further spread of the pathogen can be stopped.
Proceedings against MRSA
The treatment of MRSA intestinal causes huge expenses for health insurers, so they fight it everywhere.
In the United Kingdom, for example, it has been included in the government’s programme to reduce MRSA’s occurrence.
The program seems to be a success, according to the published data, MRSA infestations show a downward trend in England.
Priorities from the European Centre for Disease Prevention (ECDC) include the prevention of multi-resistant pathogenic pathogenic pathogens (including MRSA) and hospitalized infections caused by such pathogenic pathogens.
He wasn't a Drunken Police Commissioner who caused the fatal accident.
The official expert’s opinion has been prepared in connection with a fatal accident caused by the police captain of District XXII.
Attila Morvai, a spokesman for the Metropolitan Prosecutor of the Metropolitan Prosecutor’s Office, said Attila Morvai, a spokesman of the Metropolitan Prosecutor’s Prosecutor’s Office on Friday after an expert’s opinion had been prepared.
The expert’s opinion substantiated the initial test result, which already concluded that the former police commissioner did not drink alcohol before sitting behind the wheel.
The leader of the XXII. district precinct at that time hit the 55-year-old man who did not want to get to the other side of the voyage on the other side of the pavement.
(The latter, i.e. that the pedestrian didn’t cross the zebra, is not a mitigating circumstance based according to the rules of the Highway Code, the cornering motorcyclist still has to give priority.)
The pedestrian lost his life after being transported to the hospital.
Because of what happened, the district police chief resigned from his post and asked for his resignation, according to the police, because the incident shocked him.
Investigative Prosecutor’s Office is conducting an investigation into the case.
Attila Morvai also pointed out that the former police commissioner cooperated with the prosecutors conducting the investigation from the very beginning.
Another EUR 400 million for infrastructure
János Veres, Minister of the Finance Minister and Marta Gajecka, Vice-President of the European Investment Bank (EIB), signed three credit line agreements worth a total of EUR 400 million in support of education, transport and environmental infrastructure and energy.
The European Development Bank (EIB) approved a EUR 300-million-equity facility in 2006 for the financing of transport and environmental protection projects from the Cohesion Fund, of which HUF 100 million has already been concluded by the parties, the now signed contract is about the use of the remaining EUR 200 million.
Education-related projects are also financed by the EUR 150-million-euro loan agreement.
The EIB has approved a total of 300 million euros for this purpose for this purpose, and the remaining EUR 150 million can be used later by extending the loan agreement.
In the field of innovation, the parties have signed a EUR 50-million-euro contract, which is primarily used for the research and development project of the University of Debrecen.
Subway 4 was also discussed at the meeting.
They agreed that when Hungary finalizes the details of the financing of the metro with the EU, they will come back to whether and how to modify the original contract.
According to János Veres, this can take place in the first quarter of 2008.
Since mid-1990, the EIB has financed 113 projects with a total of EUR 8.4 billion.
The interest rate of the loans will be provided by the EIB with an 8-year grace period attached to EURIBOR and without a maintenance commission.
Bulgaria the real estate paradise of the European Union
The development of the Bulgarian real estate market is astonishing – the attention of the whole of the whole of Europe is focused on the previously mentioned Balkan country.
What, where and who are still shopping in the cheapest, but fast-expensive market in the EU?
Analysing Re/Max’s Bulgarian division.
Unbeknownst Eastern European real estate shopping destination is without a doubt Bulgaria with inexpensive ski apartments and coastal cottages.
Among the mountains are the most fashionable areas of Bansko, Borovec, Pamprovo and Vitosa, while Albena, "Golden Coast", "Supply Coast", "Supply Coast", ancient Sozopol and Nessebar are also looking for real estate around Kavarna and Balcsik, but more and more people are also looking for real estate in Kavarna and Balchiks.
According to the analysts of Re/Max Bulgaria, sooner or later the central part of the country will arouse the interest of investors, such as Veliko Tarnovo, Gabrovo, Elena, Triavna, and more and more people are also shopping in the vicinity of the extremely inexpensive Rus.
The capital, Sofia, also offers attractive opportunities, the commercial and office spaces promise the highest return.
Bulgaria is on the verge of becoming one of Europe’s most attrasting golf paradises, making the summer and winter seasons a priority destination for the other two seasons as well.
Presighted investors are already buying a lot, for example around Bansko.
According to the analysts, buyers of real estate in Bulgaria are also striving to change their lives.
Most of the buyers are English, but they come from France, Belgium, Spain, and more and more from Romania.
Maltese and Cypriot investors are also present, and there are traditional Russian buyers at the Black Sea and ski resorts.
Foreign buyers can be divided into three groups, says Georgi Jankov, the regional director of Re/Max Bulgaria.
Deplorers will either buy refurbished real estate for between 50,000 and 100,000 euros, or degraded old houses for between 5,000 and 15,000 euros and then refurbished.
Deploring good accessibility and communication facilities, it’s important for them a big city, shops and the proximity of a local doctor in general.
The second category for holiday buyers (Bulgaria was added to the list of the top 10 German holiday destinations in December).
The third group consists of business-generating investors, who are buying for medium- or long-term value growth.
The price of real estate in the capital is continuous, as there is a growing demand.
Bulgaria has the lowest property prices in the European Union, but they have grown by 21% a year in the last five years.
The increase in real estate prices basically exceeds salaries.
Fodor and the Six Hungarian Percenters
According to Gábor Fodor’s Minister of Environment and Water, experts’ meetings will be concluded on Tuesday, and on Wednesday, following the official inauguration, the negotiations of the international climate regime after the ministers will continue.
Experts and Politicians from 192-domeste of 192 countries are discussing the Bali Roadmap, which provides for efforts following the emission-reducing period designated by the Kyoto Protocol until 2012.
At present, there has been a heated debate between the developing and developed countries.
The U.S. has also indicated that it does not currently intend to deal with accurate figures, although it is inclined to agree to reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 25 to 40 percent by 2020 compared to 1990.
Another big question at the conference is whether the developed countries undertake, in addition to mitigating emissions, to provide both financial and technological assistance to the countries in need.
An agreement has also been reached on subsidies for developing countries.
The subsatisfaction fund provided for in the Kyoto Protocol will be headed by a 16-member board of directors and is expected within the United Nations Global Environment Fund (GEF).
Six per cent of Hungary
Gábor Fodor, Minister of the Environment and Water, on behalf of Hungary, supports the EU’s efforts to preserve its flagship role in global climate protection efforts and to facilitate the establishment of a new international agreement by 2009.
The Minister explained that by 2020, compared to the 1990 level, the EU undertakes a 20 per cent reduction in greenhouse gas emissions, irrespective of the success of the “post-2012 agreement”, and is ready to undertake a reduction of up to 30 per cent if other large developed regions of the world and developing countries are also willing to assume commitments appropriate to their responsibilities and capabilities.
Gábor Fodor pointed out that in 1997 the countries joining the Kyoto Protocol aimed at a total reduction of 5.2 per cent, within which the EU – at that time its 15-member member state – assumed minus 8 percent.
The obligation in Hungary is 6.8 per per cent greenhouse gas emission mitigation compared to the period 1985-87, which will be fulfilled by our country – recalls the press release of the Ministry of the Environment.
There is a high stake in Bali, as the long-distance international climate policy agreement will have to be concluded within two years, otherwise its entry into force would surely be postponed to 2012. In this case, reduction commitments under the Kyoto Protocol would not immediately be followed by a new international emission reduction system, the necessity of which was also pointed out in its latest report by the IPCC, the International Scientific Panel on the Nobel Peace Laureates for Climate Change.
Emission cuts would be delayed and an unavoidable increase in the Earth’s average temperature above 2 Celcius, which scientists say would have extremely serious and irreversible consequences.
The British army is recruited on YouTube.
Recordings of actions in Iraq and Afghan will be published on video-sharing by British military units to promote themselves.
The British Air Force (RAF) was first reaped on YouTube: special forces operating in Afghanistan have been uploading videos of their anti-Taliban actions since March 2006, the BBC reported.
These miniature, helmet-mounted cameras will be inspected at Kandahar’s Air Force’s operational headquarters and sent to London, where they will be uploaded to the Internet.
The political message is unavoidable.
1,139 recordings have already been uploaded to the Internet by the air force, and more than 75,000 people viewed their “channels”.
The U.S. Department of Defense, registered as “defenceheadquarters” in the spring of 2007, is posting videos on operations in Iraq and Afghanistan, while the British Commonwealth Office (Commonwealth Office) and Iraq’s international peacekeeping corps corps (MNFI) also have their own YouTube channel.
Expansion in Afghanistan
According to the air force’s original intent, he posted the videos for the purpose of recruiting, and according to the Ministry of Defence, it is also a good way to familiarize the general public with the real purpose of the military’s presence abroad.
“Propaganda published in this form does not cost almost anything compared to traditional television recruitment commercials, defense expert Gordon Mackenzie told the BBC, but it inevitably conveys some political message, as it certifies the legitimacy of military actions decided by the government in a special way.
The U.S. was a toy.
It is also a problem for the army that, in addition to the carefully edited and censored recordings, amateur videos are also uploaded to the divisor, which, for example, also show the death of the soldiers: in the somewhat hypocritical, “official” propaganda films the officers only talk about the losses, but they don’t present the bloody side of the fight.
There have already been a number of videos from Iraq and Afghan theatre that have been filmed on the internet, but the British army has only recently received it.
The U.S. is recruited in a slightly different way, the free-to-downship America's Army, in which the player can play the role of soldiers in a more or less victorious simulation.
