Ferret strike forces are gathering.
The Liga Trade Unions and the National Federation of Workers’ Coordinators and the National Federation of Workers’ Coordinators will set up their strike on Monday’s indefinite-scale strike on Friday.
There will be a culmination on Saturday, and on Monday – the day of the final vote on the Health Insurance Act – the work stoppage will begin.
The action will surely result in a significant disruption in transport, as rail transport will be almost completely halted, and they will also strike at BKV until 1 p.m.
On Friday afternoon, the League of Trade Unions will present the details of Monday’s strike organized jointly with the National Federation of Workers’ Councils.
There will also be a LIGA-MOSZ strike committee, which will coordinate Monday’s actions.
There will be a protest rally on Saturday, and from Monday the organizations joining the strike will stop the work indefinitely if they are unable to reach an agreement with the government.
This means that the date on the day of the parliamentary vote on the Health Insurance Legislative Act has not been set a date for the end of the strike.
If the National Assembly does not adopt the health insurance law, the trade unions will immediately withdraw the strike call.
On the other hand, if they vote in favour of the legislation, the strike committees will meet and decide to continue.
According to the agenda of the National Assembly on Monday, it is also conceivable that the health insurance law will only be voted on in the late-by-bassday, perhaps at night.
Significant disruptions can be expected due to the strike starting on Monday: work will be terminated indefinitely at the railways, and in the morning a part of the public transport in Budapest and more than half of the Volán-companies may be stopped.
Simultaneously at the same time, there will also be half-track road closures in many parts of the country.
A number of trade unions and NGOs have joined the strike call, the list of which will be continuously updated at the League’s Hoday.
Some of the organizations called for an indefinite period of strike, while others announced a temporary cessation of work.
Until Thursday afternoon, MÁV and the Free Trade Union of Railway Workers (VDSZSZ) could not even reach an agreement on a sufficient service, even though more and more people are travelling by rail as the Christmas holidays are approaching.
According to reports so far, BKV will go on strike from 1 p.m. to 1 p.m., the Trade Union of Teachers and the Democratic Trade Union of Pedagogical and Pedagogestrians will hold a four-hour strike between 7:00 a.m. from 7 a.m. to 9 a.m.
The Hungarian Physician Association is on strike nationwide, and the Democratic Trade Union (EDDSZ) works in the Health and Social Sector (EDDSZ) holding a two-hour warning strike.
The most important of the four demands formulated by the League is that the government should withdraw the proposal on the reform of health insurance and issue a new concept for a social debate.
The second demand is to gradually introduce a new method of calculating pensions due from next year.
The third thing is to provide early-retirement pensions with the possibility of getting into the system even before 2010.
The fourth reason for the action is that the Free Trade Union of Railway Workers (VDSZSZ) belonging to the Liga trade unions does not accept the offer of the Ministry of Economic and Transport regarding the railway wing lines, it expects a different offer.
Political Capital: the Hungarian Gárda increased the country’s risk
The activities of the Hungarian Guard, founded in August this year with the support of the Jobbik Movement for a Better Hungary, increased the political risks of Hungary and the region in several respects, according to the Risk Analysing Division (Risk Analysing Division).
According to the company’s “risk warning”, the biggest political risk in connection with the activities of the Hungarian Guard is that “with an aggravation of ethnic tensions between the already existing ethnic tension between the Hungarian Gypsy and non-Gypsy populations, even violent ethnic clashes may occur in the short term”.
The main reason for this is that the Hungarian Guard’s political strategy is presumably aimed at provoking such an act, the research institute added.
As they wrote, all this can be traced back to one of Hungary’s main occupiers: “the public discussion of the Roma issue was handed over to the representatives of radical and extremist political groups, who were able to build a political strategy for the Gypsy-Hungarian contradiction”.
This deepens the problem, disables normal dialogue, strengthen extremists and increases the likelihood of violent conflicts.
Moreover, according to the research institute, the activities of the Hungarian Guard “due to the great domestic and international media attention significantly impaired the image of Hungary abroad, especially in terms of faith in the country’s stability and democratic development”.
It is recalled that from the autumn of the autumn of 2006 it is part of the reorganization of radical and far--right radical and far-right-sponsored radical and far extremist groups in Hungary that from the summer of 2007 a number of “national self-defense” organizations have been established in Hungary.
“It is a common feature of these clusters to define themselves as an NGO, while openly proclaiming the preparation of their members – even armed – for protection (disaster protection, defence, law enforcement) tasks, moreover, citing the incompetence of the police, the army, i.e. the state,” they added.
They pointed out that all this is done in accordance with the method, based on the anti-political and non-partisan nature of society, it seeks various political aspirations into a civil guise.
The emphasization of civil society and the form of the association are not only used to avoid the legal pitfalls of judicial registration in the case of these associations, but also a political strategy.
According to the analysis, the activities of the Hungarian Gárda are a risk-enhancing factor from the point of view of neighbourhood relations.
“In recent months, several neighbouring countries have referred to the Hungarian Guard as a potential source of danger, and ultra-nationalist political actors in some of the neighboring countries want to justify their own anti-Hungarian policies by referring to the formation of the Hungarian Guard.
“This has an impact on the political stability of the region,” he added.
According to the research institute, the founding of the Hungarian Guard is not a unique phenomenon in East-Central Europe, so political risks have increased for the region as a whole.
The operation of the Hungarian Guard is similar, for example, to the activities of the Bulgarian National Gárda, which was announced by the far-right Bulgarian National Union (BNU) in August 2007.
The formation of the Bulgarian Guard is due to the massive Roma riots that erupted in a quarter (Kraszna Poljana).
BNU took advantage of this to decide on the establishment of the guard, saying that the organization’s goal is “national self-defense”.
“In the past 17 years, there have been “Gypsy-terror terrors” in Bulgaria, but the state has remained idle and indifferent.
“The initiative sparked a heated debate in the Bulgarian public.
An increase in the risk of ethnic clashes is indicated by some Roma organizations in Bulgaria’s announcement that they will set up their own guards for resistance.
Since the operation of the Hungarian Guard so far shows similarities with the operation of the Bulgarian National Guard, it cannot be ruled out that the Roma in Hungary also react to the threat by organising their own self-deficitance.
Attila Juhász, director of research at Political Capital, told the telegraph office that the risk analysis is one of the activities of the research institute.
As he said, they publish their risk analysis every year, and if they experience something that could have an impact on the change in the risk, they will inform the general public, as in this case by issuing a risk warning.
It's not a red-letter holiday everywhere.
In Hungary, the law speculates exactly the red-letter days, and around the festivities the reorganization of the working day has already become a common practice, of which there will be fewer next year.
There is no unanimity celebration in the EU and in the United States.
The Hungarian jurisprudence qualifies a national and state holiday and a public holiday.
The public holidays are mostly associated with church holidays and eminent historical events.
We also celebrate the New Year as well: the last day of the old year and the first day of the old year are both public holidays – the latter just to allow New Year’s gradients to be able to relax.
There are only eleven red-letter celebrations all year round.
It is also the memorial day of the revolution of 1848 on March 15 and the Memorial Day of the Hungarian Revolution of 1848.
On the 20th of August we celebrate the founding of the state, on the 1st of May we celebrate the work, and on the 1st and 2nd of November we commemorate our relatives and friends.
In addition, a two-pound holiday is Easter, Pentecost and Christmas.
According to the calendar, our state, national or even religious holidays treacherously fall for the weekend, it has become an exercise in cases where we would only work for one day before or after the red-letter holiday.
It is true that the price of the extended weekends is that we do not stay away from the workplace-school on previous Saturdays.
For many years, the Minister of Labour regulates exactly when the day before or after the holiday must be worked out in a separate decree.
The law extends to all employers and the employees they employ, public employees and those in a civil service relationship.
However, it does not affect service companies and institutes operating uninterruptily operating on public holidays due to its intended purpose.
This year the work schedule will only be modified around the end-of-the-year holidays: from the 24th of December, life in the country almost stops life, as according to the experiences, the days between Christmas and New Year are “scheduled down” by the majority of people “deposit” or use their saved holidays for this purpose.
“Next year we will have more holidays in the middle of the week and at the end, so there will be fewer workday relocations than this year.
At first, the calendar is reconstructed around the celebration of work, so that the workers can stay at home together for four days.
It will be a working day on the 26th of April, in exchange for Friday, 2 May.
The other reassignment is related to the 23rd of October: it will have to be worked on Saturday, October 18, instead of the 24th on Friday.
The end of the year, however, can be completely spent next year in the spirit of the holiday, as a non-stop break will be held almost everywhere from the 24th of December, even if it falls on New Year’s Eve.
