Sixty-two thousand people couldn’t change their licenses.
Six-hundred-thousand drivers missed the deadline and were not able to replace their old driver’s license in time, the validity of which expires at the end of the year.
“Today is the last time they can still apply for an exchange so that the offices will be able to issue a new driver’s license in good time until the end of the year.
However, the ministry is not afraid that after the New Year a 100,000 drivers will leave without a valid entitlement.
According to the authorities, the driver’s license has not been substituted primarily by those who no longer want to drive.
“While the districts represent a violent fall in interest in driver’s license exchanges, the metropolitan offices have been flooded with requests for driver’s license exchanges, for which interest this week has increased.
The mayor’s office receives approximately 2,000 clients a day.
There'll be about half an hour in queuing.
Approximately 140,000 drivers did not change the driver’s license in Prague.
A new driver's license will last 20 days.
However, a new driver's license can also be applied for with a five-day-by-a-half deadline.
In such a case, however, the driver still has to pay an extra five hundred kroner.
Drivers who will not be able to carry out a mandatory driver's license exchange until the end of the year will be penalised from January.
The culprit has to pay a maximum of 2,000 crowns.
However, a police officer can also resolve the violation by agreement.
In the case of an administrative offence, the penalty shall be 2,500 kroner.
According to earlier information received from the town hall, those who do not travel anywhere can also be fined.
Supposedly, you can pay up to 30,000 kroner.
Earlier, the ministry called on the authorities to be lenient and to impose fines of up to a thousand kroner.
According to transport ministry spokesman Karel Hanzelka, tens of thousands of fines are not threatened.
According to him, a driver’s license is a document in which people can decide whether or not they can.
This isn't an identity card that has to be valid for everyone.
People who don't want to drive should relinquish the driver's license.
It's just enough to take it or send it by post to the right office.
At the end of the end of the year, driving licences were no longer valid from 1964 to 1993.
Drivers, however, need to gradually replace the other types of driving.
Those with documents from 1994 to 2,000 can be substituted for another three years.
Driving licences issued between 2001 and 2004 will only expire in December 2013.
