The teacher appears in front of a quarantine court today because of the name given to a teddy bear
A Khartoum court sentenced the British teacher to 15 days imprisonment and expulsions from the country in Sudan on Thursday evening, his lawyer said.
The British woman was accustomed to allowing six- or seven-year-old students to name a teddy bear as Muhammad.
Such an act can be punishable by lashes.
The 54--year-old British woman, a teacher at Unity High School in Khartoum, has been arrested in her own apartment on Sunday. According to Section 125 of the Prosecutor’s Penitentiary, she was accused of insidious and rioting after the students’ parents repeatedly filed a complaint with the Sudanese Ministry of Education.
The story began in September, when the teacher asked his 6-7-year-old students to choose a name for the class teddy bear.
“Children suggested a total of eight names, including Hassan, Abdullah and Mohammed,” said Robert Boulos, the school’s principal.
In the end, 20 out of 23 children opted for the first name of Muhammad.
Of course, Muhammad is the name of the Islamic prophet, but it’s a very common first name in Sudan.
The children then made a diode together with the teddy bear's photo and "My name is Muhammad".
The teddy bear itself has not been labeled with this name. The problem lies in the fact that any depiction of the prophet constitutes a violation of Islam.
Gillian Gibbons, one of Gillian Gibbon’s Muslim colleagues teaching in the same school, said he knew him well, and that the teacher would never have done anything that would violate any kind of religious crisp beliefs. According to him, it’s just an “unimportant flaw”: “I’m just so sorry that the children voted for the name.”
Numerous pupils of the teacher agree with this opinion. According to them, the teacher "treats the children very well" and "would never talk about a religion in the class".
It should be borne in mind that Muhammad is also the second most popular first name in Great Britain, and there are 14 different spells in English.
This pre-nominal name, so often chosen by Muslim families, is a way of expressing respect for the Prophet (Friendly he receives this name for the first-born child of the family.)
But what are the rules that apply to the use of the first name?
Can you get this name, for example, a game?
It's obviously a matter of interpretation.
