The strength of the collision is well illustrated by the fact that the front of the car is dented, the windshield was cracked in the shape of a star, and the bumper was torn off.
Pontoise Attorney General Marie-Thérse de Givry said in an extraordinary announcement yesterday morning that he commissioned the IGPN and the Val-D'Oise County Directorate of Public Enforcement with the investigation, which was initiated for "fashioned homicide and non-assisting".
During the afternoon, the prosecutor said at a press conference that at the current stage of the IGPN investigation, the responsibility of police officers could be ruled out.
Prosecutors said three eyewitnesses interrogated in the accident on Sunday evening confirmed “the police report that the scooter arrived relatively quickly from the left side of the police car.”
“Yesterday, from a streak that was undoubtedly dragdened by the footrests on the road, it was shown that the two vehicles collapsed and sailed together for a while until the police car managed to brake.
The vehicles were confiscated for roadworthiness.
The bodies of the two victims underwent a medical examination yesterday.
- What happened immediately after that?
The rumour in Villiers-le-Bel yesterday spread like wildfire, according to which the police quickly left the scene without providing assistance to the two youngsters.
In this regard, the IGPN is cautious, but it excludes that the cops have made “any serious mistake”.
“It’s the hardest part of the investigation, which requires an even more in-depth investigation, and in doing so requires extra caution,” he said from a police source.
Libération encountered an eyewitness who reported a few minutes after the accident.
One of the windows of his apartment overlooks the exact location.
“With my cousin, who’s a nurse in the hospital, we immediately went downstairs trying to resuscitate them,” he said.
"We couldn't do anything."
He then spotted “a police officer who was completely confused and wailing.
“The other two tried to resuscitate one of the youngsters, and the fourth made the call.”
Tensions escalated as the hopeless situation of the two juveniles became apparent, so the arriving policemen transported their colleagues from the scene by car.
It is also clear from the eyewitness that the ambulance arrived “about 20 minutes after the police leave.”
Cardiac massage was also used, but in vain.
“I wouldn’t say the police didn’t try to help young people.
The young people did get help.
The fire brigades immediately arrived at the scene.
“They’ve done everything they can to save them,” the prosecutor said.
Tensions are rising in Pakistan
On Monday, the Pakistani police prevented the first protests two days earlier after a state of emergency imposed by General Musharraf.
According to eyewitnesses, about 2,000 lawyers protesting against the measures introduced in the eastern part of Lahore in the eastern part of the country were wounded by tear gas bombs used by law enforcement forces.
In response, they threw stones at the police, many of whom were injured.
There's at least 350 lawyers.
Similar incidents took place in Karachi and Rawalpindi, where they were attacked by lawyers and injured by police.
Several people have been produced.
Registered lawyers of the Supreme Court in Islamabad called for a general strike and announced demonstrations over the arrest of some 500 opposition members, human rights defenders and lawyers this weekend.
On Monday morning, the streets leading to the Supreme Court were sealed in order to prevent assembly.
There have also been several protests in Multan in the central part of the country and Peshawar in the north-west.
“Since Monday, the lawyers’ movement has also been backed by the powerful Muttahida Majlis-e-A-A-Amal alliance of religious parties. His leader urged the people to send flowers to judges who do not agree with a state of devoid of emergencies and who swollen their swollen before Musharraf.”
“Musarraf’s promise is no longer valid.”
Pakistan’s Supreme Court is directly affected by an exceptional measure announced on Saturday evening.
In the next few days, the Court of Justice will have to make a statement on the legality of President Musharraf’s re-election with an indirect vote on October 6.
Iftikhar Mohammed Chaudhry was dismissed and replaced by others.
“Everything we are witnessing is illegal, unconstitutional and contrary to Supreme Court’s rulings,” he told The News.
President Pervez Musharraf’s promise to remembrance from the military to play the role of civil president “lost effect” after the introduction of a state of emergency, Deputy Information Minister Tariq Azeem announced on Monday.
Musharraf had to divest himself from the military uniform by November 15, the end of his first term as president.
The vice-presitor also reaffirmed the news about the postponement of the elections in January.
The Pakistani government has refuted circulating rumours across the country, according to which Governor Musharraf would have designated a forced dwelling for his deputy.
More than one-Third Pregnant Pregnant Pregnant
This results include unsolicited (24%), or improccident predominant pregnancies.
More than a third (36-5%) of pregnancies are still unplanned, according to a study by the French National Institute of Demography (Ined), forty years after the enforcing Neuwirth law liberalizing contraceptive.
This results include unsolicited (24%), or improccident predominant pregnancies.
According to Ined, different contraceptive methods, such as twrite or uterine spirals, are still failing in 5-6% of cases.
However, according to the institute, the liberalization of contraindication has made “birth regulation more effective”. The study notes that only 5% of women between the ages of 20 and 44 in 1970 took birth control pill, but today this figure is 60%.
8 out of 10 pregnancies are now planned.
One in five plans with a couple of months of accuracy, and if possible, by spring, as this is the most favorable season to receive a child.
Spectacular repair on the space station
The astronaut Scott Parazynski and the crew of the International Space Station (ISS) had to spend more than seven hours working in space to carry out a spectacular repair of a torn solar panel essential for the station’s scientific activities.
This dangerous spacewalk proved to be a record in several respects: the repair lasted a very long time, there was very little time to design it, and it had to be carried out away from the residential modules, with "baked" tools by the astronauts.
The problem acquitted on Tuesday when the astronauts separated the Harmony module from Discovery. This is a huge cylindrical element that will soon be the site of European and Japanese scientific laboratory activities (see framed section).
This module is a kind of gateway for laboratory modules.
However, they must take care of their power supply.
The crew’s second task was to relocate one of the modules, which had already been on the ISS, with the help of the 17.5 ton solar panels, to its final location.
Subsequently, the crew began to unleash the 76-metre-wide solar panels of the module.
One of the synchronously folding boards was instigated by a trapped cable in a length of about 1 metre.
In order to avoid any further injury, the opening of the board was not resumed.
The astronauts were originally tasked with a control walk from the Discovery space shuttle, and then to repair another solar panel with deteriorated rotation systems. Instead, they had to devote their entire time to repair the damaged solar panel.
Scott Parazynski was chosen to perform the stunt hanging from the space shuttle’s 30-met-metre-strength robotic arm attached to the station’s crane.
He's one of NASA's best "space walkers."
He's tall, he's strong, he's extremely skillful, muscular on his torso and a long arm.
The 46-year-year-year-old man had to "operate" the "injudicated" solar panel.
After carefully assessing the damage, he cut the defective cable and fixed the damaged area with a few "brains".
He was also assisted in his work by another “space walker” who remained near the safe passage, as well as other astronauts, including the crew’s captain Peggy Whitson.
The tool he used, a mini hockey stick that was insulated against hundreds of volts of tension in the solar panels, was flogged by the station's astronaut's astronauts.
After finishing the operation, Scott Parazynski waited for his companions to open the board completely, and only then packed his tools and returned to the station.
Yesterday afternoon the astronauts took a half-day break, and today seven returned to the Discovery space shuttle to begin their return on Wednesday.
The EADS share price is declining
The price of EADS shares fell sharply on the Paris Stock Exchange, after the aircraft and defence equipment company announced that the protracted development of the A400M aircraft would result in an additional cost of 1.2 to 1.4 billion euros.
The EADS share price currently stands at EUR 21.96 after a decrease of 4%.
The company was compelled to reconsider its forecasts for the 2007 operating results, as the development of the A400M’s engine is delayed, which clearly delays the delivery of the aircraft.
Demand for the hand-in-command transfer of the tactical transport aircraft, announced in October, “it will cost between EUR 1.2 and EUR 1.4 billion, of which more than EUR 1 billion will affect Airbus,” the European EADS manufacturer’s parent company said in a statement.
Due to the emergence of these expenditures, the EADS will have to provide new plans for its operating profit in 2007 next Thursday, when announcing its quarter-final earnings, the company added, but did not publish any further figures.
The company group also indicated that it had taken into account a six-month delay when preparing its new cost estimate.
In 2006, EADS’ operating profit declined by 86%, largely due to the delay in the development of another program, the A380, which also puts a huge financial burden on the company.
The company originally planned to hand over the A400M to the French military in October 2009.
Resist the status quo
Sunday’s elections don’t make a big surprise: the vast majority of the Russians will vote for Putin’s party, United Russia.
This is reflected in Russia’s need for stability and arbitrary governance after a heavily manipulated campaign.
“Three days prior to the parliamentary elections, Russian President Vladimir Putin speaks to the electorate through public service channels in a televised message, which has already been recorded, but its content is still confidential.
“Putin is the leader of the Kremlin’s pro-great-great-great-great-great-great-Self-Russia list, which is expected to win an overwhelming victory in the elections.
At yesterday’s reception of foreign ambassadors, the behaviour of the head of state did not reveal anything: he only made another outburst against the interventions of the Western countries, which was a popular topic during the campaign.
“We know the cost of real democracy, so we would like to have honest, absolutely transparent and open elections without any violation,” he said.
According to the opposition, this is not the case.
The opposition condemns the campaign in which, according to them, the elector was under pressure.
The leader of the Communist Party, Gennady Zjuganov, claims that “there was not such a dirty election in Russia even in YEI’s time.”
After last weekend’s protests, former World Ches chess champion Garry Kasparov has been sentenced to five days in prison for unauthoritated protests, and according to his lawyer, he is still unable to visit.
“The Russians are still thinking of “homo sovieticus.”
Predictable electoral achievements raise two more obvious questions:
- Who's following Putin as head of state?
The official newspaper published the date of the first round of the presidential election, which will be March 2, 2008, and the nominees will be able to announce their intention to run until December 23.
Since Vladimir Putin was also elected in 2000 and 2004, his mandate could no longer be extended.
There is no other successor on the horizon.
- What role will Putin play from March?
Will you be Prime Minister?
Or as the leader of the Almighty Unified United Russia party or the parliamentary majority, he will decide on the government and the president?
Either way, the outgoing power triumphs in both the parliamentary and presidential elections.
This has now become a tradition.
“The Russians are still thinking like ‘homo sovieticus’: they don’t feel that they can control their fate,” said Lev Gudkov, a sociologist and director of the independent Levada Center.
Nikolai Koposov, the historian of the University of St. Petersburg, goes even further: according to his theory, five hundred years compared to Europe, the development of civil society is also delayed. The traditional communal spirit hindered the development of individualism.
“Putin went back to the paternalist model, which perfectly suited to the people, because this new kind of paternalism was not associated with intimidation, but with a certain economic well-being,” says Maria Lipman of the Carnegie Foundation.
Today, the Russians “reafiably reject their personal responsibility”, which was quickly revealed after the disintegration of the Soviet Union.
"Why should we think?"
The phenomenon affects all strata of society, even the business world.
The administrative elite has an interest in maintaining the current situation: the local lists of Unified Russia will be headed by 65 governors, who will come from the leaders of the 85 Russian regions.
Freedom imported from the West often embodies chaos, as happened in the 1990s.
The public prefers stability.
The Russians do not vote for extremist parties either.
Tamara Cserednichenko, a 54-year-year-year-old teacher concludes: “Why should we think?
Those in power aren’t that bad, so why should we replace them?”
Deficiant Memories
After more than 60 years of clarification, the Bad Arolsen archive, documenting the fate of 17 and a half a million victims of the Hitler regime has become public.
The 47-million-kiguest document, which occupied a 27-kilometre range of shelves in Bad Arolsen in Hessen, has so far only been researched for “humanitarian” purposes, i.e. they have only provided a unique and name-appropriated opportunity for the former victims or their descendants.
Eleven states, including Germany, France, Israel and the United States (Western States), reached an agreement with researchers and historians in May 2006.
Once all states have ratified the treaty, the archives were opened yesterday.
Confidential information
“The unquestion of the Bad Arolsen archive will allow historians to carry out “recrupulous research on the relocation of imprisoned people, the inhabitants of the camps and the state of health of forced laborers,” explains Reto Meistern, director of archives managed by the International Committee of the Red Cross.
Until now, a number of states, including Germany, have been opposed to the publicing of the documents, as they contain names and thus confidential information.
For example, the alleged homosexuality or mental defiance of some deported victims.
The records of the concentration camps and the arrest warrants issued by Gestapo began to be assembled for practical reasons after the war. The goal was to investigate civilians deported or convicted on the basis thereof, and to provide evidence of their incorporation, on the basis of which they could be compensated.
More than 300 permanent employees are currently engaged in the processing of tens of thousands of individual needs.
However, a part of the scientific community does not consider the opening of the Bad Arolsen archive to be of paramount importance.
“These documents basically contain nothing new,” said Wolfgang Benz, director of the Center for Anti-Semiticism at the Technical University of Berlin in 2006.
EU-Imney Free Trade Agreement to be signed in 2008
Indo-European bilateral commerce’s turnover is already close to EUR 60 billion per year.
The EU is India’s number one trading partner and accounts for 18% of its foreign trade.
“The European Union and India are hoping to sign a free trade agreement in 2008, European Trade Commissioner Peter Mandelson and Indian Trade Minister Kamal Nath have announced.
EU foreign ministers had already agreed last April that the European Commission would initiate negotiations to sign free trade agreements with India, South Korea and the Association of South-Esunautasian Nations (ASEAN).
The EU is India’s number one trading partner and accounts for 18% of its foreign trade.
The EU is also India’s main foreign investor, but India is only the EU’s 9th most important trading partner, according to figures from the European Commission.
Peter Mandelson explained that “a free trade agreement could have an economic stimulus effect for both sides,” and “India could increase its presence in a thriving market of 450-millions.”
“Europe is the primary or secondary importer of the ten most important products exported by India, the European commissioner added. Every year the two giants, Europe and Asia exchange “47 billion euros worth of products” and “10 billion euros worth of services” with each other, said this week’s head of the European Commission’s delegation in Új-Delhi.
(Source AFP)
Responsiastics: The Bank of England is donating 10 billion pounds to the financial market for a five-week maturity
The Bank of England intends to alleviate unsatisfactories in terms of lending conditions, so it announced on Thursday that it will pump 10 billion pounds into the financial market, which will be made available at a base rate of 5.75% this time this time with a base rate of 5.75% this time this time.
“We intend to alleviate concerns that financial market conditions will be particularly bad at the end of the year, and we also want banks to plan their liquidity position more securely for this period. Therefore, a significant part of the Bank’s reserves will be offered on the international financial market as part of a five-week-long open-market operation from December 6,” the bank said in a press release.
According to the bank, EUR 10 billion (EUR 14 billion) will be put on the market on 6 December, at 12:15 a.m. local time. The central bank’s base rate is valid for the offer and the repayment deadline is 10 January.
This is the first time that the Bank of England has issued liquid funds for five weeks in the context of an open-market operation.
The duration of such operations is usually a week.
In a press release, the Bank of England added that it is ready to take further measures in order for inter-bank loans to be aligned with the central bank’s base rate.
The current inter-bank interest rate was 5.829% on Wednesday.
The three-month interest rate surpassed the level of 18 September: it currently stands at 6.6%.
“Since the beginning of the summer financial market crisis, the Bank of England has commissioned a significant punitive interest on the amounts pumped into the financial market, which deterred the banks from their use.
According to the rumour (strengthened by the development of the pound’s exchange rate compared to the euro), many people would have preferred the resources offered by the European Central Bank, to which they would have been able to obtain with more favorable interest rates and greater discrepancy.
The UMP and the NC refuse to examine the socialist proposal on purchasing power
The UMP and the Nouveau Centre on Thursday refrained from discussing the articles of the socialist bill on purchasing power, which is equivalent to the rejection of the text itself.
Negotiated at the meeting initiated by the socialists contained about ten measures adopted by the socialist party’s national office on Tuesday, such as a 50% increase in the employment surcharge, the reintroduction of variable fuel taxes, the introduction of travel cheques and the restriction of rent.
Since the UMP and NC voted 180:115 against examining the articles, the meeting between the governing party and the opposition, which sometimes resulted in sharp-sounding arguments, was interrupted only a few hours before President Nicolas Sarkozy’s televised address on purchasing power.
Jean-Marc Ayrault, the head of the socialist delegation, announced that he would resubmit all of his group’s suggestions within the framework of an amendment to the corrective draft financial law, which will be placed on the agenda of the National Assembly next week.
A new festival is born.
There have been a number of rumours circulating about the new music festival in philadelphia organized by C3 Presents. Now, however, the company that organizes and promotes the event has announced that the Vineland music festival will be held in New Jersey in collaboration with British Producer company, Festival Republic.
“It’s an honor for us to work with someone who’s initiator of today’s U.S. festival,” Charlie Jones told Billboard.com.
The C3 also organizes the Lollapalooza festival in Chicago and the Austin City Limits music festival.
The Vineland Festival in New Jersey will take place in the summer, so there will also be a way of camping.
The festival will take place from 8 to 10 August, in a 500-crown private area, about 40 minutes from Philadelphia.
The director of the Festival Republic, formerly known as Mean Fiddler, is one of the world’s most respected festival organizers.
In 1989, he began organising the British Reading Festival, which attracts around 80,000 visitors every year.
In 1999, Melvin Benn further expanded its palette with the organization of the Leeds Festival, and in 2006 the Festival Republic established the Latitude Festival at Henham Park in Suffolk.
Since 2002, Melvin Benn has been a co-owner of the Glastonbury Festival’s manufacturing, licensing and organisation. This festival can now accommodate up to 177,500 people.
Charlie Jones of C3 called the Glastonbury Festival “the most inspiring event” he had ever participated. He added that his company and Melvin Benn’s company were working in parallel to enrich the region with a European-style festival event.
“We’ve already selected the location in philosophia, and together with the Fairmount Parks Commission, we’ve been working hard to organize this festival in central Philadelphia,” Charlie Jones said.
“We have been conducting activities with the city, and the Commission has expressed great enthusiasm for our plan.
“Unfortunately, due to the political manoeuvres of the city’s leadership, the organization has failed,” Charlie Jones said.
“Fortunately, we’ve been able to talk about this with Melvin Benn, and now we’re preparing to host a huge festival in another unique location that ultimately serves the interests of the residents of Philadelphia.”
C3 Presents resurrected the Lollapalooza festival in 2005 at the Grant Park in Checago after proclamating its exceptional festival organizer with the Austin City Limits music festival in 2002.
The gross revenues of the two events in 2007 amounted to USD 9.8 million and USD 11.3 million, respectively.
Both festivals took place in the city.
“Melvin Benn’s European concept is based on the rural festival, while our festivals usually take place in urban green areas, says Charlie Jones.
“Our goal is to combine the two models in some way in some way, creating both the rural atmosphere in Europe and the urban lifestyle in America.”
Vineland is about halfway between Philadelphia and Atlantic City.
“Vineland’s people and even the mayor are enthusiastic about supporting the event,” says Charlie Jones.
“While you don’t talk about the details of the agreement, you’ll say that “the conditions are favorable enough to make it possible to invest in such an event at a location that is hopefully hosting the festival for a long period of time.”
According to Charlie Jones, the festival will be “husiastic” in size with a capacity of 80,000 at the Bonnaroo music festival in Tennessee.
The ensembles have already been invited to the event, but at the time of the press release, nothing has ever been final.
“The Vineland will basically be a rock festival, but we’re designing a variety of musical offerings to make sure everyone finds something to their liking,” says Charlie Jones.
Jones and two of its partners, Charlie Walker and Charles Attal, together make up C3, which at the latest Billboard Touring Awards received the "best independent event organizer".
Montennian to confront Terminator
“It’s not easy to lure Hollywood directors to Montreal if you have to deal with the Terminator itself...
"Sleeze" is one of the tasks of the Montreal delegate.
The mission promises “politically difficult,” as California Governor Arnold Schwarzenegger opposes filming his own production abroad.
It’s really hard to get involved with the “Terminator-governor?”
