Will there be year-end rally on the stock market? Expert opinions will be shared by the “subprime” crisis
Just five weeks before the end of the financial year, investors are already rubbing their palms and can’t wait for the usual end-of-the-year incident.
This year, however, it is unclear for experts whether this will actually happen.
According to Banif, “statistically, the stock market generally behaves positively in December.
However, projections with only statistical backgrounds are not necessarily correct, in fact, in the current situation the market is characterised by uncertainty, at least in the short term.”
Experts don’t be in favour of the end-of-the-year flucency of the stock market, although it states that “the stock market is maintained by the presence of liquidity, which provides an opportunity to invest, because there is a shortage of guaranteed alternatives, in particular the fixed yield, which has a negative effect on the escape towards quality securities, as well as in the short term”.
As a result, the stock market will be characterised by a lateral, volatility-free movement, “especially since the impact of the Fed interest rate reduction is still unfamiliar, the price of crude oil is high and there’s also a threat of inflationary tension”.
Javier Barrio (BPI’s sales manager in Spain) agrees, so it would also be risky to say that there would be a rally.
According to the expert, “the situation isn’t rosy at all.”
Ibex’s loss was nearly 4%, compared to the highest figures recorded on 8 November, when the index closed at 1,945.7 points.
In spite of this, not all experts are so pessimistic.
Juan Solana, an analyst at Intermoney, Ibex 35 could close the year closer to 16,000 points due to the reorganization of the ministries, as well as due to payments to the usual pension fund.
On the other hand, the expert adds that there will be some changes this year, as the rally will be postponed until the second week of December, the week after the long weekend concerning the Constitutional Celebrity.
The analyst considers it improbable that volatility would influence market trends.
Federico Olucha, an analyst at Inversis Banco, said: “We are close to the technical level, and “because we can see a downturn in the stock index, which may also rise depending on the news coming from the U.S. regarding the consequences of the credit market crisis.”
Analysts are confident that they will be favorable.
Analysts will agree on the securities aimed at achieving Ibex’ new maximum quotas.
Solana from Intermoney emphasizes that “it’s the turn of the big securities this year.”
“The expert would put the two big banks and some construction companies on the list, “since the electrical sector will be very expensive.”
Federico Olucha from Inversis Banco states that the increase is due to the banking sector and the industrial sector.
The rally can also be positive for the construction companies, of course, if there is at all.
“The ACS has been trying to regain its positions for several months and we hope that Ferrovial and FCC construction companies will follow this path,” he said.
Cyclic consumption will be the protagonist of the rest of the year due to the increase in sales during the Christmas sales period.
On the other hand, according to the expert, technology companies can also benefit, although there is not too much representation in this area on the Spanish market.
Fifter five times in seven years
Over the last seven years there have been only two drops in the exchange rate in December.
The real year-end rally could be observed in 2003, when the stock marketes recovered from the crisis caused by the bursting of the technological bubble.
In the same year, when Ibex corrected 35, 28.17 per cent, a 6.68 per cent jump was recorded in December.
So it’s still open whether 2007 will be the fifth profitable year in a row (Ibex has an 8.36% increase since January) and whether the rally will happen.
The Banco Popular Espa ?ol 200 opens a 12-hour drawer from Monday to Saturday
The Banco Popular Espa ol decided to refurbish most of its accounts.
This concerns a total of 200 accounts located in the most commercially affected parts of Spain.
These branches run under the name Popular Max and make a big difference for the bank.
But for the rest of the Spanish institutions as well, as this is the first group, whose branches are regularly open until 8:30 p.m. until 8:30 p.
The other group, BBVA, which has also repeatedly insisted that it plans to open its branches with extended opening hours, has so far not yet taken the necessary steps.
However, he is about to reach an agreement with the trade unions, according to which he would close 75% of his accounts in exchange for one afternoon, though not for the public.
The experiences of the Banco Popular Spain will be of decisive importance for the other institutions, as on this basis it will be decided whether they will join an initiative to support the more favorable opening hours.
That's exactly the objective of the Banco Popular Espa?ol project.
They would like to target shopping individuals, small and medium-sized enterprises, sole proprietorships and local businesses.
The bank intends to operate its 200 branches throughout Spain for 18 months with the already mentioned opening hours, as it is not about a new opening hours, but a refurbishment.
To do this, the institution modernizes its branches located in the trading zones according to the needs of the customers of each street.
In such a way, the extension of the opening hours is accompanied by a new concept of the nature of the drawers, a more modern, more colourful design.
Responsible of commercial banks, Ángel Rivera, points out that the reorganization does not require a large investment, but they have to employ more employees, which means eight or nine employees per branch instead of the current six.
The branches, in which negotiators designed for SMEs will also sell non-financial products.
Facilitating experience
Banco Popular Spain has already carried out the initiative in its three branches in Madrid.
According to Ángel Rivero, the experience was favorable.
Drawers in Bravo Murillo, O’Donnell and Gran Vía Streets in Madrid have been chosen, and Rivero said the decision was correct.
There are currently 2,500 accounts for Banco Popular.
A further 60 operate under the name Mundocredit, specialises in immigrants and also has a very favorable opening hours, opening even on Sundays and public holidays.
PopularMax does not use the BBVA model, where some drawers offer a wide range of non-financial products.
“We don’t want to create competuation for our customers,” Rivero points out.
