More fears about the Russian-Ukrainian gas dispute
There has been a recurrence between Ukraine and Russia, which could affect Russian fuel to be transported to Western Europe.
A large part of Russian gas exported to the European Union (EU) will be routed through Ukraine, and the disputes between the two nations will always attract a lot of attention.
Kiev has stated that if Moscow raises Ukraine’s gas prices, they will also impose higher transit costs on Russia.
As a result of an earlier dispute that erupted last year between the two countries, supplies to EU countries have declined.
The most recent controversy emanating between Kiev and Moscow relates to an attempted agreement between the Ukrainian government and the Russian state-owned natural gas monopoly Gazprom on the amount of the gas price payable by Ukraine to Russia in 2008.
Gazprom has stated that it will have to make a 50% increase compared to the price agreement concluded with Ukraine in relation to gas extracted from neighboring Turkmenistan by 2008.
According to the analysts, this could represent a 40% increase in the price increase of the shipment from Gazprom for Ukraine.
“It’s important to argue in accordance with one of the most important natural gas-transfused transit states during the talks,” Ukrainian Economy Minister Anatoly Kinah told the Reuters news agency.
“Champions in relation to gas prices should definitely be linked to our debate on the cost of transit through Ukraine (and heading to Western Europe).
As a result of the most recent major gas price dispute between Moscow and Kiev, Gazprom closed the gas tap to Ukraine in January 2006.
This resulted in a domino-like decline in exports to Western Europe, which at the same time pushed up prices to a large extent.
Russian gas accounts for a quarter of the European Union’s supply of gas.
Russia has the world’s largest reserves of natural gas, but most of them are still unexplored.
Subsequently, Gazprom is supplemented by natural gas from Turkmenistan and other former Soviet countries in Central Asia.
