It's not Warhol's crates.
The one who connects the concepts of Sweden and the wooden crate with each other thinks of Ikea, not Andy Warhol.
Or is it?
At present, wooden cranes manufactured in Sweden cast a dark scoundrel on Warhol’s works.
Most of the ninety-four "Stockholm-type" Brillo Boxes listed here are forgeries.
After a few months ago questioning the authenticity of Andy Warhol’s 1968 Brillo Boxes, the internal investigation of Moderna Museet in Stockholm came to a sobering result: the six "Brillo Box" copies donated to Moderna Museet by Pontus Hultén, a former museum director in 1995 to Moderna Museet - and it's very likely that a large number of "Brillo Boxes" in the international art market.
This prehistory is an embarrassing chain of long-complicated artistic and trustees.
Warhol in New York in 1964 he created his first "Brillo Boxai" painted wooden crates based on the red-blue-and-white design of the cardboard box "Brillo".
Hundreds of Brillo crates made of cartons were exhibited at an exhibition in the Moderna Museet in 1968 in Moderna Museet in 1968: “For cost-saving reasons), they did not come from Warhol (for cost-saving purposes), they were transported directly from the Brillo carton factory.
It wasn’t about works of art, it’s about their templates and simple exhibition pieces.
At the same time, Warhol gave permission in 1968 to be produced in Sweden, but it was not exhibited at Moderna Museet.
The witnesses of the time are now talking about about fifteen cranks that, as the then-certified Original-Andy-Warhol-Brillo-Brillo-Box under the designation "Stockholm-type".
Some have been auctioned for more than USD 100,000.
The problem, however, is that Pontus Hultén, the legendary museum director later, falsely claimed that in 1968 a hundred wooden crates had been manufactured and exhibited in Sweden, which were then abandoned by Warhol.
In fact, Hultén’s 105 crates were only produced in Malmö in 1990 for an exhibition in St. Petersburg.
Warhol was killed three years ago.
Lars Byström of Moderna Museet also examined three privately-possicced boxes in addition to the six Brillo Boxes owned by the museum, two of which were made in Stockholm in 1968: The pressed chipboards were first de-founded and chanted, after which the white oil paint was used with a brush.
On the other hand, the structure of the copies from 1990 reveals that the dye was transported directly onto the chipboard with a paint cylinder without foundation - in addition, it's not an acrylic dycoady, but an acrylic paint.
“These crates weren’t authenticated by the artist, so they would have to be erased from Andy Warhol’s Brillo Boxes official list,” says Lars Nittve, director of Moderna Museet: “Unless the Andy Warhol Art Authentication Board in New York makes a different choice, Moderna Museet will classify the six crates in its collection as a copy/exhibition.”
The Authentication Board, of course, had already announced in June that it would look into “serious allegiances” in connection with soap powder crates.
Since then, however, there has been an unbeatable silence.
